  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nThe weather will continue to remain active through the rest of\nthis week with a longwave trough over Canada and a broad ridge\nout west. Later this afternoon and evening, a weak boundary \nwill kick off some isolated showers and thunderstorms across\nmainly northeast PA, and possibly clipping into portions of the\nSouthern Tier. Although instability may be limited with some\ncloud cover hanging around this early afternoon, MLCAPE values \ncould rise to around 1000 J/kg, with 0-6 km bulk shear values\nbetween 30 to 40 kts. What is lacking is mid-level lapse rates,\nand given this, confidence is low for strong storms today, \nthough a couple storms could become organized and have gusty \nwinds as the main hazard, with a secondary hazard of potential\nlocalized excessive rainfall.\n\nThere was some concern of some overnight convection, but\nconfidence is decreasing as ingredient parameters for good\ndevelopment is just not there after sunset. High pressure will \nbuild back into the region tomorrow, leading to a warm, dry \nday. The next weak disturbance will move through overnight and \nsupport isolated to scattered showers. With limited instability,\na couple thunderstorms will also be possible, but stronger\ndevelopment is unlikely. Conditions will then dry again Thursday\nmorning but with a deepening upper level trough, the next round\nof showers and thunderstorms develop during the afternoon. \nShowers will continue during the overnight hours as a cold front\ndrops south across the region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, the end of the week and most of the \nweekend will be dry and cooler. After a week of temperatures maxing \nout in the upper 70s to mid 80s, most of the region will see highs \nin the 70s heading into the weekend. The next system to monitor\nwill be a weak low pressure system to move into the region by \nSunday night with the next round of showers and thunderstorms. \nDrier air is quick to follow this system, though wrap around \nmoisture may be enough to support some lingering showers on \nMonday.",
